foreign  [Music]  Dr Alika LaFontaine is an  anesthesiologist with 20 years  experience in medicine but for the next  year he's got a new job  president of the Canadian Medical  Association the largest national  advocacy group for doctors  on a recent trip to BC Dr lafontaine's  colleagues at Royal Columbian Hospital  in New Westminster made him feel right  at home in the operating room  a far cry from his days in medical  school a time he says was so difficult  he almost walked away from his dream it  was very intimidating for me coming into  medical school without having any  medical background on either side of my  family my mom's a first generation  immigrant my dad comes from a small  farming community in Saskatchewan so  ever since we were young we didn't have  a lot but we we always had Big Dreams I  sit here now as the head of an  association that's been around for more  than 155 years you know there's more  than 92 000 Physicians across this  country  and we all share a belief that we can  make a difference in people's lives I  think the the scariest part of what's  going on right now when it comes to  Physicians and providers is that  they've lost hope  would you say Canada's Health Care  system is collapsing  I would absolutely say that it's  collapsing and for a lot of us  we hear about this collapse but it  doesn't affect us directly  and  when you actually are in the health  system where people are waiting you know  10 20 hours to get into an emergency  room or when someone puts an  advertisement into a newspaper hoping  that they can connect with a family doc  you can't help but acknowledge that we  are at a very very different place than  we've ever been in healthcare  why is Canada's Health Care System  collapsing the crisis was decades in the  making right I think we often look to  covet as you know the triggering event  but in reality whether it was covet or  something else there was a stress that  was coming on the system these decisions  we've made over the years weaken the  link between patients and providers and  I think if we rethink the way that we  design care around that again I think we  have a lot of Hope of making our way  through this stress  many Canadians are experiencing that  stress through a lack of access to  doctors long waits for treatment and  Hospital closures  but doctors and nurses say their mental  health is worse now than ever more than  half of Health Care Professionals are  experiencing severe burnout  when you hear from people that you know  they're either scaling back the amount  of clinical time that they're going to  do whether you're a physician or a nurse  or other Allied Health provider or  they're thinking of leading medicine  entirely because of the stresses and  burden of what they're experiencing day  to day you know that the need for that  isn't just urgent it's  it's something that you have to do right  now one of the most amazing things for  me to witness was how a generation of  Canadians became armchair  epidemiologists and if we can include  Canadians in this conversation where  they start to understand health  care in a way that they didn't before  I I think we'll move that conversation  in the direction it needs to go how do  you think that approach could help  there's two things that I think can help  immediately you know the the first is  breaking down those silos that we have  eventually so we can actually start to  share information people and resources  in a much more collaborative and  coordinated way the second part is  making sure that we move towards true  team-based care and so Physicians have a  role nurses have a role you know  Pharmacists and other members of the  healthcare team have a role and it's  respecting that we're all trained  differently we all do different things  well I want to ask you question in the  context of that 2020 report called in  plain sight that talked about systemic  racism in BC's Health Care System but  experts have said you could essentially  apply the findings of that to every  Province and territory how do you begin  to tackle such an ingrained issue like  that one of the insights that I've had  over the past 20 years is that  racialization isn't about the person  sitting across from news about yourself  it's about how you see the people that  come into  the places that you provide care and  what you're willing to accept as far as  lived experience and evidence you know  the experience of racism is real I've  experienced it myself I have family  members who've experienced it  organizations like the Canadian Medical  Association that have been around for  155 years  you know they're on their own path  towards reconciliation  and there's a lot of positive things  happening right now you know we've never  had more indigenous physicians in this  country than we've had now and once we  can acknowledge that things are real we  can then work towards those solutions  that I think are are in the hands of the  people that experience them undoubtedly  as the first indigenous president of the  Canadian Medical Association you are a  role model does that come with added  pressure I've had the opportunity to  stand on the shoulders of a lot of  amazing people  and I have to be ready to have people  stand on my shoulders you know I I'm not  going to be able to solve these problems  by myself  and if you look at leadership as a  process where it's not about your voice  but amplifying the voices of people who  are actually creating change  that weight doesn't feel as heavy  [Music]  to a group is something Dr LaFontaine  has experience with and you may have  even heard it before  he grew up singing and playing music  with his siblings  performing in front of Canadian  audiences as the fifth generation a  touring band  and that family bond is important to him  I have amazing wife as well as four  amazing children you know my kids were  so excited when they found out that I  had been selected by Alberta Physicians  to become this year's CMA president  medicine  is a type of job where you give a lot  but you get a lot as well through  everything I I still have that  connection with my wife and my kids you  know I come home they're excited to see  me and they look at me and  you know I really feel like they know  that I see them  you know and and I'd say that if you're  trying to change anything like the thing  that you should always be most proud of  is that the people that you care about  they know how much you care  I think I sense some emotion coming up  as you talk about that it must mean a  lot to you to know that your family  knows you see them  what piece of advice or wisdom would you  want to make sure you instill in them  it's this idea that when you connect  with people like you can change the  world we often talk about about systems  and you know processes I mean we're  we're a hyper operationalized Society I  think the average Canadian sometimes  looks at leadership in medicine as very  disconnected with with what they're  going through  and it is true in a lot of ways that  when you come into the system you feel  faceless but at the end of the day it  really comes down to people  and connecting with people because I  think if you see each other  clearly and you start to care about each  other again you can't help but change

